9) on background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4a21e3da/D3/3/code.py:21:    which still implies c_split = 9. The later ray drawing and piece motion are unchanged.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D6/1/code.py:155:    - Each projectile still travels 5 cells from the apex, but the odd-distance samples of that flight become visible: steps 1 and 3 are painted maroon (9) whenever those cells are background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D4/4/code.py:19:#    The added rule is intentionally as light as the source task 291dc1e1: it keeps the original\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D6/2/code.py:10:#     new: Every projectile paints its whole 5-cell flight path. Distances 1-2 use color 4,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D5/0/code.py:62:#   - Segment count is slightly reduced relative to the original seed, and ray intersections are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D5/0/code.py:91:    - The trigger pixels still mark where beams split, but the ray color is no longer copied from the trigger itself; it is determined by the trigger's position in the sequence along that blue diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4a21e3da/D12/1/code.py:81:    - split-line fg pixels remain 7, and rays remain ctrl-colored\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D13/0/code.py:60:#   two same-colored pairs. Outputs consistently highlight two routes, not one.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D13/0/code.py:66:#   the two highlighted routes legible instead of collapsing into one tangled shared corridor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D6/1/code.py:69:#     - No new output color is introduced beyond the original green and grey highlights.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D8/0/code.py:48:    - Segment count is capped at 3 and off-diagonal distractors are slightly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/195c6913/D2/0/code.py:315:    - A separate 2x2 block of another color marks the collision-highlight color source.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D11/2/code.py:80:#   - output ignores that dust and still highlights the same shortest route\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4a21e3da/D7/2/code.py:31:#   split-line、ray、region、corner-slide 都只能基于 `work_pts`。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D5/1/code.py:87:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D10/2/code.py:75:#    \"the unique shortest route between the two special-colored nodes is highlighted\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D10/2/code.py:76:#    but now highlighted stations are hollow green frames instead of filled green blocks.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D4/2/code.py:396:    to preserve the original family feeling of slight border irregularity.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D5/2/code.py:76:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D16/2/code.py:32:# - I reduce distractor counts slightly so the new one-sided behavior is not buried under clutter.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D2/1/code.py:76:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D3/0/code.py:231:    Key insight: Triggers are still beam splitters, but the important new constraint is that rays now treat existing structure as hard blockers instead of tunneling through it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D18/0/code.py:62:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D2/3/code.py:118:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:16:#     - two intended diagonal endpoint pairs, one dark blue (1) and one light blue (8)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:18:#     - optional unpaired dark/light blue distractor pixels and off-diagonal magenta distractors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:27:#     - difference: the output now contains both the dark-blue diagonal and the light-blue diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:43:#     - every generated puzzle contains both dark blue and light blue endpoint pairs, so the solver must\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:67:    Connect dark-blue and light-blue diagonal endpoint pairs separately, then emit magenta rays from triggers on those diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D11/0/code.py:71:    - Blue-family endpoints appear in two visible shades: dark blue and light blue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D18/1/code.py:93:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D8/0/code.py:97:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:77:# - The outer shell stays rectangular for readability; the smaller shell is slightly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/332f06d7/D8/0/code.py:57:#     the nearer square slightly inset, and then verifies the new solver output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D1/0/code.py:86:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D6/0/code.py:103:def _split_canvas(g: np.ndarray) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int] | None]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/3/code.py:30:#       then the magenta at (6,4) is the splitter. The emitted slash ray starts from (6,4), so one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D7/2/code.py:66:    - Magenta is no longer a transparent trigger. It is a blocker-and-splitter: it marks where the blue diagonal termi